ng that  happens in lawsuits and trials all the  time I've seen it many times where  lawyers argue with a judge digging  straight into their arguments when the  judge doesn't really have any idea of  what they're talking about whereas if  you give some background and argue a lot  of your substantive points upfront then  the more nuanced points that you might  make later on will actually resonate  with the judge who has a million things  on their docket and the the tactics that  Adam Schiff and some of the other  Democrats took on the first day of this  Senate trial sort of harken back to that  particular trial tactic that instead of  simply arguing the mere procedural  points which they eventually did  especially with Chuck Schumer they took  that opportunity to start making their  case in front of the senators and the  American people so whether you liked  that tactic or not whether you thought  it was pure grandstanding or you thought  the Democrats were making some good  substantive points it has its basis in  classic litigation and trial tactics to  be able to get your story out at every  Avenue that you can and not just be  cabined into the specific issue in front  but to be able to talk about all of the  issues at the same time because of  course if you have a theme you want to  reiterate that theme or that narrative  as many times as you can hoping  it will stick and of course it was  interesting to compare the Democratic  narrative which went into the  substantive issues with the narrative  put out by the lawyers for the president  who it appears were prepared to argue  for the substantive points but perhaps  not all of the substantive arguments on  day one which leads me to the next part  of this impeachment trial that really  struck a chord with me as a practicing  lawyer was the marathon nature of this  particular proceeding this may be a  feature not a bug when you're in trial  the judge sets the schedule and there's  a reason why often trial starts at 1